WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.870
Film Courage: Keith, do you come from a line
of filmmakers?

00:00:01.870 --> 00:00:02.870
Keith Sutliff: No.

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:03.870
No, I don’t.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.880
I’m the first one actually (in my family).

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.070
Film Courage: Okay.

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:12.061
So I just find it fascinating that you had
sort of this business vision along with a

00:00:12.061 --> 00:00:17.060
creative vision to go to AFM [American Film
Market] with a script that was not finished

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:18.060
yet?

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.060
Keith Sutliff: Correct.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:22.150
Film Courage: And already trying to find distribution
for it.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:24.430
That’s pretty amazing.

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:27.080
Where are you getting that…those ideas from?

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:32.610
Keith Sutliff: You know…American Film Market
is usually in November of every year in Santa

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:33.610
Monica, California.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:40.000
And when I went there I was in the process
of writing THE MASON BROTHERS and I had two

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:47.920
other projects with finished scripts that
I was trying to get off the ground.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:55.570
However, one of my good buddies in the business
(also a director and a producer).

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:58.940
He recommended to me prior to that (a couple
of years ago)…he’s like “Hey, you have

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:00.150
to go to the American Film Market.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:05.549
This is the place you have to go for meeting
distributors and it’s just great networking.

00:01:05.549 --> 00:01:06.850
You’ve just got to go.”

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:09.290
He was just telling me how it worked and stuff.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:14.700
He kind of mentored me and gave me advice
on how the whole process worked, and walking

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:21.840
to the different hotel room suites and meet
with the distributors and stuff.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.539
He kind of pitched the idea to me.

00:01:26.539 --> 00:01:28.279
This is a business, that’s what it is.

00:01:28.279 --> 00:01:33.130
It is creative, but in my opinion it is more
business that it is creativity in a sense.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.760
I mean it is creative but it’s business
is what it is.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:42.399
So you make a film (especially a feature film)
your goal should be obviously you want to

00:01:42.399 --> 00:01:43.399
make a product.

00:01:43.399 --> 00:01:47.209
You do what you love and you’re passionate
about it but also try to make some profit

00:01:47.209 --> 00:01:50.020
off of it, if you can.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:54.590
It’s just smart to go some place like a
like the American Film Market or some other

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:58.679
market to try and sell a film or anything
you have from your production company, or

00:01:58.679 --> 00:02:01.210
other projects which you are trying to get
started.

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:03.429
So it’s just wise to do that.

00:02:03.429 --> 00:02:09.119
I was just thinking with a business mind that’s
what should be done and my friend was telling

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:11.659
me to do that, so I did it.

00:02:11.659 --> 00:02:15.329
Film Courage: Do you see other filmmakers
around you, maybe the ones you went to film

00:02:15.329 --> 00:02:18.900
school with or people that you’ve talked
to here in Los Angeles that have that same

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:19.900
mindset?

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:25.790
Because some people get very offended thinking
that their work of art is also a product?

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:30.500
And I can see how it would make people mad,
but in actuality it’s true, it’s a product.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:33.200
Keith Sutliff: Right, that’s just what it
is.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.870
Some people have that same mindset but some
people don’t but they have a lot of their

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:40.719
own agenda of how they want to do things.

00:02:40.719 --> 00:02:45.420
Most filmmakers that I’ve met, a majority
go to film festivals to try to get distribution

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:49.780
for their films or just some type of exposure.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:56.129
Yes, a lot of people have that mindset but
maybe some more than others.

00:02:56.129 --> 00:03:00.330
Some people treat [filmmaking] as more of
a business and some don’t.

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:05.220
Some hustle harder and trying to get their
film distributed or some kind of pre-sales

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.690
deal prior to shooting it.

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:10.180
It really just depends on the individual.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:12.480
I’ve seen seen different people do it different
ways.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:20.859
But me, I think American Film Market and going
there to get some type of distribution deal

00:03:20.859 --> 00:03:21.589
prior is important.

